  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d71300ae98c6c03d8138ce9b0575961366eca764bc38fb290eb120e764d28b14.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?",
  "worked_solution": "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in these regions.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in this region.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/44419413221f356bac454f1aa070e3888a8ea6921db3f7c69033e7b4347f46ec.png)",
